CAT = "s65Equity"; SEG = "EQU"
Q = [
("A company pays a 5% stock dividend. Immediately afterward, the total market value of a shareholder's position is:", 'About unchanged', ['5% higher', '5% lower', "Up by the new shares' value"], 'A stock dividend gives more shares but cuts the price per share in proportion, so the total value and ownership share stay the same. It is generally not taxed when received.'),
('A preferred stock pays a dividend that resets each quarter with short-term rates. Its price tends to be:', 'Fairly stable', ['Very sensitive to rate changes', 'Tied to the common stock', 'Fixed at par by law'], 'Because its dividend adjusts to market rates, a floating-rate preferred has little interest rate risk, so its price stays close to par.'),
('An executive received restricted stock from her employer in a private placement. Before selling, she must:', 'Meet a resale restriction', ['Register as an agent', 'Convert it to preferred', "Get her firm's approval only"], 'Restricted securities are bought in unregistered offerings and cannot be resold freely until a holding period passes or an exemption such as Rule 144 applies.'),
('An employee exercises incentive stock options and holds the shares. For regular income tax, the spread at exercise is:', 'Not taxed at exercise', ['Taxed as ordinary income', 'Taxed as a capital gain', 'Subject to payroll tax'], 'An ISO exercise is not a regular income tax event if the shares are held, but the spread is an AMT preference item. A qualifying sale later gives long-term capital gain.'),
('When an employee exercises nonqualified stock options, the spread between the market price and the exercise price is:', 'Ordinary income', ['A long-term capital gain', 'Tax-free', 'An AMT item only'], "With NSOs the spread at exercise is compensation, taxed as ordinary income and reported on the W-2. That amount becomes part of the shares' cost basis."),
("An analyst studies a company's earnings growth, management and balance sheet to estimate its value. This is:", 'Fundamental analysis', ['Technical analysis', 'Quantitative trading', 'Sentiment analysis'], 'Fundamental analysis values a company from its financial statements, industry and management. Technical analysis studies price and volume patterns.'),
('A technical analyst would pay closest attention to:', 'Support and resistance', ["The company's debt ratios", "Management's experience", 'The dividend payout ratio'], 'Technical analysts read charts: trends, moving averages, support and resistance, and volume. Ratios and management quality belong to fundamental analysis.'),
('A stock will pay a $2.40 dividend next year, which grows 3% a year. The investor requires 9%. Using the dividend discount model, the stock is worth:', '$40', ['$26.67', '$80', '$20'], 'Constant-growth DDM: value = next dividend / (required return - growth) = $2.40 / (0.09 - 0.03) = $40. Dividing by 9% alone gives $26.67.'),
('A private company sells its shares to the public for the first time. This is:', 'An initial public offering', ['A follow-on secondary offering', 'A private placement', 'A tender offer'], 'In an IPO the company first offers its shares to the public. A secondary offering is a later sale, by the company or by existing holders.'),
('A special purpose acquisition company raises money in an IPO in order to:', 'Buy a business not yet named', ['Run a specific operating business', 'Lend to small companies', 'Hold a fixed bond portfolio'], 'A SPAC is a blank-check company: it holds IPO proceeds in trust while it searches for a private company to merge with, so investors do not know the target at the outset.'),
('A futures contract obligates the buyer to:', 'Buy at a set future price', ['Pay only if prices rise', 'Receive dividends on the asset', 'Buy at the market price today'], 'A futures contract is a standardized obligation to buy or sell an asset at a set price on a future date. An option gives a right, not an obligation.'),
('Compared with buying 100 shares of a stock, buying one call option on it:', 'Risks only the premium', ['Gives voting rights', 'Never expires', "Pays the stock's dividends"], "A call buyer's loss is limited to the premium, but the option expires and carries no voting or dividend rights. Leverage cuts both ways: a small price move can wipe out the premium."),
]
